  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1121 AM CST THU NOV 12 2026\n\nThe initial cold front is moving through Central AL this morning\nand will continue southeastward through the state by this\nevening. A secondary frontal boundary is expected to push\nsouthward into our area late tonight into Friday morning, which\nwill bring much cooler temperatures for Friday. Temperatures\nacross the northernmost counties and especially in Marion,\nWinston, and Walker Counties might drop into the low 30s and\napproach freezing just before sunrise on Friday. However, I think\nthe wind speeds should prevent widespread frost tonight in those\nareas, though I could see scenarios where sheltered locations\ndevelop some frost early Friday morning.\n\nFriday will be cool and dry with at least a slight breeze in the\nmorning leading to wind chills in the upper 20s to low 30s across\nthe northern half of the area for the morning bus routes and\ncommutes. Daytime highs will likely top out in the mid 50s.\n\nSaturday morning will be one of the coldest mornings we've seen\nso far this season with widespread freezing temperatures expected\nacross Central AL. Lows will drop into the upper 20s to low 30s\nareawide. How quickly the temperatures drop Friday night into\nSaturday morning will depend on when the winds decrease, but\nnevertheless, Saturday morning is expected to be near or below\nfreezing for most locations. This will likely end the growing\nseason for many areas in Central AL, especially across the\nnorthern counties and the Black Belt region.\n\nLow level high pressure will remain in place through the weekend\nand into the first half of next week limiting any meaningful rain\nchances. Our daytime highs will moderate slightly by midweek back\ninto the low to mid 60s, but overnight lows will likely stay in\nthe 30s to low 40s for most locations.\n\n25/Owen",
